Say why a process died, and stop narrating the pages that were let through.

Two changes to the console, for the same reason the bootstrap trace became
switchable: what is printed is not what is wanted, and what is wanted is
buried under it.

The first boot that got past fsck started launchd, and launchd started iOS:
SpringBoard, backboardd, mediaserverd, logd, fairplayd, xpcproxy. Then all
of them died, were restarted, and died again, and the console said only

    Process[1017] crashed: SpringBoard. Too many corpses being created.

which is the corpse allocator refusing to make another one. It is a
consequence of the crashes, not a reason for any of them, and the reason
was never printed: launchd prints an exit reason, but only for pid 1 and
only on its way to a panic.

Every process carries one. os_reason has a namespace and a code, and a
string description in its kcdata that launchd_exit_reason_get_string_desc()
already knows how to find -- that function exists in this file and is used
for pid 1. Namespace 6 is dyld, whose codes distinguish a missing dylib
from a missing symbol and whose description names which one. That is the
difference between a daemon that cannot find a library and one that
segfaulted, and it is one line per dying process. exittrace=0 turns it off.

The second change is the flood it would otherwise appear in. With
cs_enforcement_disable=1 every page of every unsigned binary takes the
"allowing (remove VALID)" path in cs_invalid_page(), and every one of them
prints. A boot that reaches launchd produces thousands, each wrapping to
three lines on a 720-pixel console with no scrollback. They report that the
thing that was asked for happened.

Only that case goes quiet. A page that is denied, and one that comes with a
SIGKILL, still print: those are refusals, and a refusal says something that
was not already known. csverbose=1 brings the rest back.

And then the line that says a process died of a signal turned out not to be
enough, because the thing it does not say is where.

backboardd aborts on every boot. What it aborts on is in its crash report,
and on this handset there is no crash report to read. The kernel allows five
corpses in flight; backboardd and SpringBoard are both in a crash loop and
keep all five; ReportCrash only ever sees a corpse, so it writes nothing.
The reports that do exist on the data partition are all from one quiet boot
hours earlier, and every one of them is the same assertion -- which is
exactly the question, since the whole point is to find out whether it is
still that one.

The backtrace needs none of that machinery. The thread that took the signal
is still here and its user state is saved where the kernel can read it, so:

    regs = find_user_regs(current_thread());
    pc = get_saved_state_pc(regs); lr = get_saved_state_lr(regs);
    for (fp = get_saved_state_fp(regs); fp; fp = frame[0])
            copyin(fp, frame, 16), print frame[1];

On arm64 a frame is two words -- [fp] the next frame, [fp + 8] the return
address -- and copyin fails safely on a bad one, so a corrupt stack ends the
walk rather than the kernel. The chain is required to climb, which ends a
loop. Twelve frames, one line, printed while it happens:

    crash: backboardd[329] sig 6 pc 0x... lr 0x... bt 0x... 0x... 0x...

These are addresses, not symbols: nothing in the kernel knows where the
binary was loaded. That is enough to work with, because the distance between
two frames inside one binary survives the slide. The assertion that has been
killing backboardd since the first boot puts its two frames 0x163A0 apart,
in every report of it, whatever the slide was that day. If the next boot
prints two backboardd frames that are still 0x163A0 apart, nothing has
moved; if it prints anything else, it has, and the new numbers say where to
look.

exitbt=0 turns it off. exitbtname=backboardd narrows it to one process,
which on a console with no scrollback and four daemons crash-looping is the
difference between reading the line and watching it go past.
